---
import Legal from "../layouts/Legal.astro";
import { CONTACT, DELETION } from "../site";

const sections = [
  { id: "what-we-store", title: "What we store" },
  { id: "cookies", title: "Cookies" },
  { id: "logs", title: "Logs" },
  { id: "where-it-is-kept", title: "Where it is kept" },
  { id: "who-else-sees-your-content", title: "Who else sees your content" },
  { id: "email", title: "Email" },
  { id: "export-and-deletion", title: "Export and deletion" },
  { id: "contact", title: "Contact" },
];
---

<Legal title="Privacy" description="What Tempra stores, where it goes, and how to remove it." sections={sections}>
  <p>
    This covers Tempra at tempra.dev, the landing page and the app. Tempra is in private beta. Questions go
    to <a href={`mailto:${CONTACT}`}>{CONTACT}</a>.
  </p>

  <h2 id="what-we-store">1. What we store</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Your account: email address, handle, theme and email settings.</li>
    <li>
      Your libraries: pages, files, comments, and the record of every change with who made it.
    </li>
    <li>
      Agents and apps you connect: their names and what they may reach. Tokens and secrets are
      stored only as hashes.
    </li>
    <li>Sign-in sessions: when they started and were last used, and your browser's user agent.</li>
    <li>How often each page is read, as counts. Readers are kept as hashed keys, never shown.</li>
    <li>Notifications waiting for you, and the mail we send you.</li>
    <li>Usage counts (requests, writes, emails) to apply limits.</li>
    <li>If you request access: your email address, when you asked, and your IP address.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="cookies">2. Cookies</h2>
  <p>
    The app sets one cookie, which keeps you signed in. It is sent only to tempra.dev and cannot
    be read by scripts. It ends when you sign out, after 30 days unused, or after 90 days at
    most. There are no analytics, advertising or third-party cookies, and the landing page sets
    none.
  </p>

  <h2 id="logs">3. Logs</h2>
  <p>
    Like most web services, our hosting records requests and errors to keep the service running
    and to fix problems. A record can include the time, the address asked for, the result, your
    IP address and your browser's user agent. These logs are kept for a short time and are not
    used for anything else.
  </p>

  <h2 id="where-it-is-kept">4. Where it is kept</h2>
  <p>
    On Cloudflare, which hosts and runs Tempra and sends its email. Data is encrypted at rest by
    Cloudflare. The person who runs Tempra can read stored data, and does so only to run the
    service, fix problems or handle abuse.
  </p>

  <h2 id="who-else-sees-your-content">5. Who else sees your content</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      People you share a library with see its pages, comments and history, including who made
      each change. Handles appear in the app's addresses.
    </li>
    <li>
      Agents you connect read and write what you allow. What their provider does with it is
      governed by that provider's terms.
    </li>
    <li>
      @tempra, Tempra's own agent, is off unless it is turned on for a library's owner. When it is
      on, a comment that mentions @tempra, or replies in a thread it answered, sends that thread
      and the pages it reads to answer to Anthropic's API, which writes its reply. Anthropic
      handles that data under its
      <a href="https://www.anthropic.com/legal/commercial-terms">commercial terms</a> and
      <a href="https://www.anthropic.com/legal/privacy">privacy policy</a>.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>We do not sell your data, show ads, or use tracking scripts.</p>

  <h2 id="email">6. Email</h2>
  <p>
    We email sign-in links, invitations to libraries, notices about your account, and, unless
    you turn it off in your account, mentions and replies in comment threads. If you requested
    access, we send one email when you are let in.
  </p>

  <h2 id="export-and-deletion">7. Export and deletion</h2>
  <p>Export any library at any time. {DELETION}</p>

  <h2 id="contact">8. Contact</h2>
  <p>
    Write to <a href={`mailto:${CONTACT}`}>{CONTACT}</a> to ask what we hold about you, to
    correct it, or to have it removed.
  </p>
</Legal>
